  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nA cold front moved across eastern Nebraska this evening,\ncurrently hung up across far southeast Nebraska. Temperatures\ndropped behind the front with most of eastern Nebraska and\nwestern Iowa now in the mid-40s to mid-50s. Looking at the \nbroader upper-level pattern, we have a ridge over the Central \nCONUS this evening. A trough stretches from the Montana Rockies \nsouth into Nevada and far southern California. A strong nearly \nmeridional Jet streak is in place stretching from New Mexico \nnorth through the Dakotas. \n\nOvernight into Sunday we'll see a developing Low over eastern\nColorado ramp up southerly flow over the Southern and Central\nPlains. This will push the cold front back north as a warm\nfront, bringing warmer temperatures back into eastern Nebraska \nand southwest Iowa for Sunday afternoon. While highs stay in the\nlow 50s for our far northern counties, highs across southeast \nNebraska will be in the upper 70s to near 80. Rain chances also \nget pushed back north mostly into South Dakota for much of the \nday on Sunday. \n\nThe developing surface low travels along the frontal boundary \nnortheast across central Nebraska Sunday night into Monday, \nincreasing rain chances again for northeast Nebraska. Meanwhile \nthe rest of eastern Nebraska and southwest Iowa mainly sees high\nclouds with occasional breaks of sunshine through Monday. The \nopen wave bringing this low pressure system through shifts off \nto the northeast as another Aleutian Low pushes in to the PacNW.\nAs this trough moves across the Northern Plains, we'll see \nanother round of potential showers and storms on Tuesday \nevening across more of our area. Even with these systems moving\nthrough our frontal boundary from the weekend stays fairly \nstationary, occasionally dropping south then shifting back \nnorth. This will keep our temperatures fairly steady through the\nweek with cooler highs in the 50s and 60s over NE Nebraska and \nhighs staying in the 70s to near 80 across southeast Nebraska \nand southwest Iowa.\n\nAnother system dropping south out of the Gulf of Alaska, riding\ndown the West Coast toward the end of the week will set up more\nsouthwesterly flow across the western CONUS. This builds up\nridging over the Central US trending temperatures slightly \nwarmer toward the end of the workweek. Weak shortwaves will \nkeep periodic showers and storms in the forecast with no \norganized threat for anything severe in sight.",
